{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1323053-revenant-mage-and-ghoul-merit","id":1323053,"title":"Revenant Mage and Ghoul Merit","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-07-08T14:29:56-07:00","id":1323053,"title":"Revenant Mage and Ghoul Merit","content":"Can the Ghoul Merit from the Book of Secrets be used to create a Revenant Mage?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>When will you rage?","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1405-anteros","name":"Anteros","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1405_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"661"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-07-08T22:48:43-07:00","id":1323130,"title":"","content":"There's nothing that really hints that it could/should/couldn't/shouldn't but I'd allow it IF the mage picked a Revanant Ghoul family and took a flaw related to the family. Stat wise it doesn't change anything, the merit in M20 doesn't say shit like &quot;You can spend blood to do X Y and Z&quot; it just gives you, the equivalent, of Fortitude 1/Potence 1/Celerity 1 (I think the celerity shit is always active too so if anything its better than reg celerity 1)<br /> <br /> Semi-Edit:<br /> If you don't use Vampire rules you only have Potence/Fortitude 1 and can't really use blood points, but my first bit still answers the question","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25315-jihelu","name":"Jihelu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"167"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-07-19T22:25:53-07:00","id":1326198,"title":"","content":"Revenant is a 7 point merit in Blood Treachery p. 79. They're meant to be npcs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1973-saint-michael","name":"Saint Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1973_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"183"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-05T12:15:43-07:00","id":1329475,"title":"","content":"Sorry to revive this even if it is on the first page, but I was waiting for approval and I still wanted to share my thoughts. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Anyhow, the original prohibition against Revenant-Mages and Ghoul Mages from Blood Treachery was basically: &quot;It's your game if you want, but this is a bad idea for REASONS.&quot; It was a warning against permitting an easy path to powergaming and other rule abuse as opposed to interesting character and story development. And there are good reasons to heed this caution. On the other hand, a powergamer will potentially disrupt things by definition, independent of any one particular 'path'. A powergamey human mage is not much more of a problem than a Revenant-Mage, since the problem is the powergaming and the person doing it.<br /> <br /> By contrast, someone who puts in the effort and imagination to construct an interesting, balanced character concept shouldn't be an issue (If you're goal is a balanced, interesting character, then you'll put as much effort into the downsides as well as the upsides.) Someone who isn't interested in balancing the obvious imbalance or in creating an interesting rationale for the character doesn't deserve it. If they're willing to work with the Storyteller and other characters to make it work, why should it be forbidden?<br /> <br /> Since the primary challenge is 'balancing' such a potentially unbalanced role the rest of this post are the ideas I've had for balance. First off, many of the limits for Ghoul Mages in 'Blood Treachery' are equally applicable to the Revenant including:<br /> <br /> - Blood addiction of the Avatar: In a sense this would be a weaker version, but it would be more <i>persistent</i> (You can't get the Revenant 'off' it, but it might take longer.) Rather than the Mage being able to survive their Avatar ratings in feedings, you might figure 1-5 years per dot in Avatar, depending on how generous you want to be. Alternately, you can raise Arete OR gain one new sphere for every point in the Avatar rating (but not both). Or the ST can simply decree the Revenant starts out addicted which would more effectively limit the potential of the mage character.<br /> <br /> The stronger Avatar still is the same risk for the Revenant as Ghouling, and may 'force' the Mage to seek out vampire blood.<br /> <br /> - The Revenant can never restore quintessence by any means except Vampire blood. They regain it the same way a Revanant might regain blood points, but that's it.<br /> <br /> Once the 'grace period' mentioned above ends (However its handled), the mage cannot raise their Arete at all (or automatically if your ST wants to go this route) or gain spheres.<br /> <br /> - The addicted Avatar and its Revenant are a beacon for 'evil' forces (Nephandi, demons and other corrupt entities, etc.) to try and address its cravings. If you consider (as below) that the Revenant-Mage is a target from both the Vampiric AND Magical societies, this isn't a trivial concern.<br /> <br /> - The Revanant's blood poisons the Avatar and it will eventually destroy itself. It can survive Avatar + Arete ratings in years, x4 for the Revenant's reduced aging. after this the Revenant inevitably loses 1 point from Avatar rating until its zero, then Arete until the revenant is gilguled. Given that even with the 'extended' span of time the Revenant would still live less than a century (and the last third of that in greatly declining power) - and probably much less given a lower Arete/Avatar score and limited ability to raise it (Small wonder Blood Treachery notes a Revenant-Mage might live to see 40 before gilgul!)<br /> <br /> - You still have to take the 7 point 'Revenant' Merit, plus whatever additional flaws the Storyteller may inflict on you to reflect the unholy union this represents. Dark Fate and nightmares were mentioned, but others like Echoes seem like they would also be appropriate (Flaws from VtM would also be appropriate)<br /> <br /> In addition to many of the limits a Ghoul mage operates under, you could add other. We know from certain merits (Shattered Avatar, Twin Soul) that Avatars could be broken up. We know from the Tremere that being Embraced will destroy your Avatar. Maybe 'half dead' states like a Revnant aren't enough to kill it, but fragment it (Avatar starts out weaker deliberately - say a max of Avatar 1 at the start.) And you'd still have to pay for the merit of course. Buying Twin Souls may be utilized in some way to 'stabilize' or slow down the Revenant's decline whilst at the same time bringing other complications with it (at the Storyteller's discretion.) The interesting thing about this approach to 'limits' is that it works more to create narrative reasons for that power which may or may not be achieved, as well as the built in drawbacks/dangers the hybrid faces (the progressive disintegration of their Avatar and magickal talents for one...)<br /> <br /> We also know from the Tremere example that you can apply 'True' magick principles to more static/linear type magic (which could include Vampiric Thaumaturgy) so an 'awakened' Revenant may have to do the same (reflecting some extremely time consuming, and costly, learning to develop any real power.) This could reflect as an inherent, across the board 'sphere-inept' for example, but it could also mean they can't easily be trained by any of the formal 'traditions' without extra time and effort put in to 'translate' those methods so they are more appropriate to the quasi-vampiric nature of a Revenant (Hermetics who have Tremere connections might be the easiest.) Of course, finding someone willing to do this (or not destroy the abomination) is a challenge in and of itself. Considering the time limit a Revenant-mage already operates under before Gilgul, this isn't a trivial concern.<br /> <br /> Lastly, Blood Treachery's cautionary tale that Awakend Revenants should be so utterly rare as to be impossible, which hints at them being extremely 'Vulgar' and thus more vulnerable to Paradox (and this may extend over into Vampiric disciplines as well, reflecting increased difficulties as the two 'sides' of the Revenant's nature war with each other.) They may operate under more obvious limitations like Conditional magic, crucial component, Sleepwalker, etc. This could also tie into the 'conflict' between Vampiric and Awakened natures. Or one could just decree a Revenant-Mage's workings automatically qualify as Vulgar and/or just institute a permanent, minimum Paradox rating that the Revenant must operate under.<br /> <br /> Those are just <i>mechanical</i> limits too. Narrative limits (which would compound WITH the mechanical limits translating in-character) can be even bigger. Such a character is likely to be seen as a threat, abomination, or both in both the Vampiric and Mage societies. We know from VTM that the actions of one Revenant could doom an entire family (making them a pariah not only from other Vampire clans, but their own family). Dracula's actions caused the purge of the Basarab Revenant family in VTM. Mages are likely to see them as an affront (like the Tremere would be), or something likely to be a Marauder or Nephandi (making it harder to get access to the training you need, finding allies, etc.) And let's not forget most Revenants are socially backwards, inbred, and largely inhuman creatures to begin with and their experiences in the outside world could be quite limited.<br /> <br /> Any of those limits could apply by themselves, or in combination, as necessary to 'balance' the obvious advantages of a hypothetical Revenant-Mage.<br /> <br /> If any of that is too complicated/potential for abuse, there are still yet other ways. Sorcerer (Revised) had an interesting merit called 'Struggling awake' where a sorcerer or hedge magician might - occasionally - pull of True Magick. That could apply to our hypothetical Revenant too without being a full-on mage and would incorporate much of the same rationale above without the complexity (have the 'struggling Awake' merit apply to a Revenant-Mage learning Vampiric Thaumaturgy rather than Hedge Magic Maybe they achieve one discipline/path instinctively, but they need to learn/research/adapt others to make it work.)<br /> <br /> One could further surmise that a Revenant-Mage could achieve more than one dot in Thaumaturgy (with a willing teacher and the required experience) due to their awakened nature. If you're really generous, you could assume that applies to any possible discipline, but that might be going too far (especially not without appropriate drawbacks to balance it out. 'Stronger Revenants or Dhampir' was already a challenge. One possible source for limits to counter would be the KOTE Dhampir, who can gain one extra dot per century of existence, but suffer lossts of health levels and attributes due to aging.) On the other hand, Struggling Awake + built in one-dot limits for a Revenant also work just fine as a way to curb powergaming at the Storyteller's discretion. The only requirement is HAVING some kind of Thaumaturgy to work off of (and in some ways this is MORE of a limit than a mortal sorcerer with the same merit would face!)<br /> <br /> I don't think these are 'perfect' solutions (there are probably things I've missed or am overlooking) and may not work for everyone (especially given how complicated some of the ideas can be!), but I present them more as a starting point people can work/adapt from to suit their purposes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25533,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25533-mister_dunpeal","user":"Mister_Dunpeal","date":". 08-05-2019, 03:20 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25533-mister_dunpeal","name":"Mister_Dunpeal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"308"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-05T16:54:03-07:00","id":1329534,"title":"","content":"Everytime I see a suggestion for 'Yeah they eventually lose their Magick' I immediately think 'Alright then I'm swapping it from Merit to Flaw'.<br /> <br /> If the ST is so worried about abuse just don't use the rules from Vampire at all. They get what the Ghoul merit mentions in M20. One extra damage success on physical hits, and I think a dice of soak.<br /> <br /> Without using any other rules, that's it.<br /> <br /> It's already 7 points and the game is over here trying to destroy your soul.<br /> <br /> If you want it to be more difficult to learn Magick and what not for thematics, sure that'd be fun and cool. Your Avatar battling against it's borderline addiction (Then again, it might not be addicted to the Blood this way, I think only one family of Revenant's have tasty, yet useless, blood?) or darkish nature. There are liches running around and Nephandi bent on destroying the world that can raise their Arete just fine (The Liches actually can't but they can learn Spheres)<br /> <br /> &quot;- The Revenant can never restore quintessence by any means except Vampire blood.&quot;<br /> <br /> That seems needlessly complicated and harmful. The Revenant's whole deal is they don't /need/ Vampire blood to have their Quasi immortality.<br /> Just make it so they can only use negative Resonance'd Tass/Areas for Quintessence. It doesn't necessarily make them evil but like begets like and would probably have an effect on their personality.<br /> <br /> And it explains why these inbred assholes are so eccentric.<br /> <br /> &quot;They may operate under more obvious limitations like Conditional magic, crucial component, Sleepwalker, etc. This could also tie into the 'conflict' between Vampiric and Awakened natures. &quot;<br /> Me likey<br /> <br /> <br /> &quot;Or one could just decree a Revenant-Mage's workings automatically qualify as Vulgar and/or just institute a permanent, minimum Paradox rating that the Revenant must operate under.&quot;<br /> <br /> Me no likey. Like I said, a Revenant Mage is small news compared to like 90% of the bullshit going on in the world. Even the Unnamed could pull off a Coincidental effect.<br /> <br /> What do you think about Half-Vampire mages? I've lumped them into the same group as Revenants seeing as they are pretty much the same except the Half-Vampire lacks a clan flaw.<br /> (Have one I'd like to play some time who's whole paradigm is just 'Mimicing my dads abilities')<br /> <br /> I don't really support the 'They can learn more than 1 dot in spheres' (Could Revenants go to 2 naturally?) unless you're using Vampire rules and probably the Struggling Awake Merit.<br /> <br /> Struggling Awake is a cool merit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25315-jihelu","name":"Jihelu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"167"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-06T10:11:01-07:00","id":1329617,"title":"","content":"Personally I've never understood why there's such an extreme bias against ghoul mages, on a purely balance level those couple of dots in disciplines are pretty useless for all but the most minmaxed of characters.<br /> <br /> It seems to me that the real problem is that one of the writers/game designers has a problem with them and has abused their position to screw this option over.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11490-dogstar","name":"Dogstar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11490_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"381"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-06T11:08:10-07:00","id":1329622,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dogstar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1323053-revenant-mage-and-ghoul-merit?p=1329617#post1329617\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personally I've never understood why there's such an extreme bias against ghoul mages, on a purely balance level those couple of dots in disciplines are pretty useless for all but the most minmaxed of characters.</div> </div> </div> </div>Even for those players, of all the min-max options that Mage have if you play with Merits and Deffects, Ghoul Mage doesn't stand out a lot.<br /> <br /> When you could be an Immortal unkillable by normal damage or be a Nephilim and have 7 dots of Advantages (which can be more potent than lvl 1 Disciplines) on top of easy Quintessence, the advantages of being a Ghoul aren't that impressive. Enough to justify it's cost as a Merit, for sure, but not nearly OP enough to be the &quot;wet dream&quot; of min maxers (as the Authors say)<br /> <br /> It doesn't justify turning that Merit into a Dark Fate. Especially not when the condition already has many disadvantages (Like, say, being addicted to vampiric blood that can enslave you, and having a Beast) <hr /><br /> Sooo...Revenant Mages. Truly they avoid some pitfalls of being a Ghoul (not needing vitae). Hence the Merit has to cost more (7 or 8 freebies sounds right to me). Making harder for them to raise Arete it's reasonable, but condemning them to progressive weakening and Gilgul it's not fun.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 08-06-2019, 02:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-06T14:11:09-07:00","id":1329651,"title":"","content":"I think 6 points for Revenant is fine.<br /> <br /> Ghoul is a 5 point Merit ( I think I keep saying 7 so ignore my dumb ass) and gives you Potence 1. I rechecked, and it doesn't even give you fortitude 1 + Celerity 1. That's only if you use the Vampire rules and spend EXP on them.<br /> <br /> Ghouls have the benefit of, possibly, living forever. (Assuming Vampire doesn't yeet you out the window)<br /> <br /> Revenants avoid this 'I need Vampire blood' Issue but have a lesser version of the unaging feat. They also have a flaw. I think this little bonus is only worth like, 1 extra point. Especially since they are going to be having a harder time anyway (Family issues, Vampires, etc.) Though you could just make it 7 anyway and easily take the corresponding 'my family is crazy' available flaws to cut the cost down.<br /> <br /> I'd even buy that you have to take some sort of flaw associated with your Revenant status. Not as a 'Fuck Revenant Mages' type of thing but as a 'Revenants are all inbred and are fucked somewhere, take a flaw relevant to your family'. Not something as crazy as Dark Fate, but essentially the clan flaws the families have.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25315-jihelu","name":"Jihelu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"167"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-06T16:35:02-07:00","id":1329663,"title":"","content":"There are new rules for Revenant Mages in The Black Hand: A Guide to the Tal\u2019Mahe\u2019Ra that are way better than those in Blood Treachery. Though for some halfassed reason the rules, which are meant for a modern game, are compatible with Dark Ages: Mage but the conversion where necessary should be easy.<br /> <br /> Main things.<br /> 1. You get the Revenant Family Weakness and their access to Disciplines and other normal Revenant qualities.<br /> 2. The downside is that your Arete, or Foundation, is limited to your Humanity/Path rating -2.<br /> <br /> The number 2 is for Revenants only and fo regular Ghouls the downside is that they cannot raise their Arete/Foundation at all while Ghouled.<br /> <br /> The upside for both is that Magic that fits Vampiric legends as know by mortals os treated as coincidental.<br /> <br /> More or less it works as above, I do suggest checking the book yourself though as my memory can be faulty sometimes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1629-possessed","name":"Possessed","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1629_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1140"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-09T16:47:20-07:00","id":1330167,"title":"","content":"I will admit that while a neat concept. I have never understood how a mage dipping into being a ghoul to become a second rate vampire while stunting their growth as a mage by splitting XP expenditure between the two paths is somehow powergaming compared to the almost literally anything else solely investing in Magery could grant them.<br /> <br /> But then, I often got the Sense that the OWoD writers have no idea how to play tabletop games and much prefered writing lore.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11744-enginseer-42","name":"Enginseer-42","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"774"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-09T21:26:01-07:00","id":1330204,"title":"","content":"I don't understand because of that reason AND from the reason that Ghouls are limited to ONE POINT in the discipline (Unless you use an optional rule FROM A VAMPIRE BOOK that says if you have blood of a LOW GENERATION VAMPIRE you can learn more) [Caps to indicate why these are not issues]<br /> <br /> OWoD writers are strange.<br /> M20: Hey guys, never ever play a Nephandi ever. Beat them up for being bad people!<br /> M20 five and a half seconds later: In this player example, so and so (A Nephandi) has just had a Quiet episode and her ST....<br /> <br /> Towards the end it seemed they were so concerned with low powering Mages with requirements it just extended to everything that /sounded/ strong.<br /> Ghoul mage? Can't have that, lose your Magick<br /> Immortality Merit? Psh lets just make it five points.<br /> Nephelim? Fine with me.<br /> Want to have a specific paradigm that's been considered somewhat okay? (Chi stuff) Well now you need to use prime 2 and a bunch of other taxes to do what other Mages can do.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25315-jihelu","name":"Jihelu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"167"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-10T07:52:28-07:00","id":1330247,"title":"","content":"Yeah the treatment of Ghoul/Revenant Mages has always been rather odd, considering the cost of Ghoul Merit itself and the fact that you cannuse low cost Merits to build a \u201dbetter\u201d Ghoul Mage with all the same advantages and non of the downsides.<br /> <br /> The greatest advantages Ghoul Mages get?<br /> 1) Slow (if Revenant) aging and fully stopped aging for salongin as you have a supply of Blood.<br /> <br /> Can be beaten by a 1 point Merit with no downsides.<br /> <br /> 2) Access to Disciplines. Mostly 1st level only, physical only unless aside from Family Disciplines (Revenants) or Domitor Disciplines and higher only if Domitor is low generation. Aside from Blood bond as a disadvantage these Disciplines cost 20xp for the 1st level and 15xcurrent for the rest if allowed.<br /> <br /> Many 1st level Discipline effects can be either replicated by Coincidental Magick or low cost Merits. Non of the downsides.<br /> <br /> 3) Finally one big advantage is that Magick that looks like traditional Vampire tricks is considered coincidental. This can be quite a big deal considering things associated with Vampires as it will allow some mind effects and even some transformation effects Etc.<br /> <br /> This is the major benefit that cannot really be replicated easily with other Merits but is it still worth the limitations to your Arete, Blood Bond and other disadvantages?<br /> <br /> So no Ghoul/Revenant Mages aren\u2019t, atleast in my personal opinion, overpowered even without the limit to Arete. Blood Bond and the general Vampire connection is likely quite enough of a disadvantage to Balance it out. But if you wish to use the official Ghoul/Revenant rules the Black Hand book is the way to go.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1629-possessed","name":"Possessed","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1629_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1140"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-10T08:43:46-07:00","id":1330257,"title":"","content":"The disciplines also cost blood, something you have a very low bloodpool for. Not all Domitors give blood out like candy, too. Some give you 1 blood point a month just to keep your ass in stasis.<br /> <br /> Revenants don't have to deal with this as much because they get 1 a day but regular ghouls have like, a pool of 2.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25315-jihelu","name":"Jihelu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"167"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-10T11:31:09-07:00","id":1330280,"title":"","content":"Honestly it's not just their decision as to WHAT was overpowered or power game... But how that should be handled as well.<br /> <br /> For example. Forged By Dragons fire had the rule &quot;You can only craft Charms during actual play. Never in time skips.&quot;<br /> <br /> Basically, the image given was everyone else playing and having fun while that DIRTY POWERGAMER sat in his lab working.<br /> <br /> In actual play, I've never seen that happen. Instead. Everyone sat down and did crafting. We would do our rolls then move forward.<br /> <br /> It was like they designed the system. Decided it was OP and then flipped off anyone who tried to use it rather than actually fixing it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11744-enginseer-42","name":"Enginseer-42","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"774"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-12T15:21:43-07:00","id":1330632,"title":"","content":"Revised era Mage overall looked like playing according to Jess Heinig's house rules for his own street level Hollow Ones/Orphans campaign to me, so it's not surprising that trying to pull in elements from another game line was regarded as BadWrongFun and punished severely regardless of how relatively little effect being allowed to do so would have on game balance.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-13T22:00:58-07:00","id":1330879,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Matt the Bruins fan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1323053-revenant-mage-and-ghoul-merit?p=1330632#post1330632\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Revised era Mage overall looked like playing according to Jess Heinig's house rules for his own street level Hollow Ones/Orphans campaign to me, so it's not surprising that trying to pull in elements from another game line was regarded as BadWrongFun and punished severely regardless of how relatively little effect being allowed to do so would have on game balance.</div> </div> </div> </div>Imagine thinking theme and game design are separate things in a World of Darkness game.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Writer, Game Designer, Pro since 99.<br /> <br /> Thoughts at <a href=\"http://mobunited.com\" target=\"_blank\">mobunited.com</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/164-malcolm","name":"Malcolm","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar164_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"306"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-17T10:22:09-07:00","id":1331351,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jihelu</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1323053-revenant-mage-and-ghoul-merit?p=1329534#post1329534\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Everytime I see a suggestion for 'Yeah they eventually lose their Magick' I immediately think 'Alright then I'm swapping it from Merit to Flaw'.</div> </div> </div> </div>Eh. I don't think it's that big a problem by itself - it depends on how you handle it. Even if the particular game spans centuries it's not insurmountable. Heck part of the appeal for that is I see certain parallels between the quest for ascension and VtM's 'Golconda' quest. A struggle between the awakened avatar and the vampiric curse seems like it could be approached in a lot of interesting ways. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If the ST is so worried about abuse just don't use the rules from Vampire at all. They get what the Ghoul merit mentions in M20. One extra damage success on physical hits, and I think a dice of soak.<br /> Without using any other rules, that's it. </div> </div> </div>Also the 'unaging' quality. It's basically the vampire version of the Fae Blood and kinfolk merits, plus certain other flaws already associated with drinking vampire blood. But I'd say sticking with just the Mage Rules (or Vampire rules if you're running a Vampire-centric game) rather than trying to link the two is also the LESS complex option, which I saw as the major issue with my ideas (its alot of book-keeping.) And as you note you can 'simulate' a ghoul/revenant by mage rules (or a mage by Vampire rules) without much effort. I also like Hunter's approach to mages, and you can easily adapt Sorcerer (like I said) in various ways as well (Even without Struggling Avatar. Sorcerer revised is kinda interesting in the permutations it offers.) Kindred of the East's dhampyr 'Joss' has certain parallels to coincidental magic as well and could work with adaptation. I think Werewolf might have too, but I was never that into Werewoofs. <br /> <br /> I'm going to compress the rest of your response to my ideas together to save space and note that, again, I expected most ideas to be a 'YMMV' situation. You're bound to get disagreement about whether something is overpowered or not for various reasons. There's just too many variables at play, and many of them involve the storyteller as the player(s). That's why I can understand in principle why such limits exist, but at the same time its a context-sensitive issue and gets dealt with case-by-case. <br /> <br /> Basically let the Storytellers and players decide what will or won't work for them. Don't try to shame them into not doing it. And Blood Treachery really just seems to run right into 'shaming'.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What do you think about Half-Vampire mages? I've lumped them into the same group as Revenants seeing as they are pretty much the same except the Half-Vampire lacks a clan flaw.<br /> (Have one I'd like to play some time who's whole paradigm is just 'Mimicing my dads abilities')<br /> I don't really support the 'They can learn more than 1 dot in spheres' (Could Revenants go to 2 naturally?) unless you're using Vampire rules and probably the Struggling Awake Merit. </div> </div> </div>Do you mean 'Dhampir' or are we talking Thin blooded? To me there's a certain crossover between Dhampir/Revenant/etc and Thin Bloods at that point.<br /> <br /> On the Dhampir/Revenant front, I think they should be allowed to go beyond one dot like Ghouls can, but having read some of the past debates on 'more powerful Revenant/Dhampir' I can see some of the objections given the thin blooded. I guess it depends on how you balance it, again. KOTE had Dhampyr being able to reach level 3-4 in disciplines due to age (and suffering penalties due to that age) so I think its possible to balance. I also know Danislav Revenants could get certain Garou gifts up to level 3 or 4 (wheras regular Kinfolk only learned one level IIRC - go figure) so level 3 or so seems a nice balance point for 'hybrid' characters whether its discipline, spheres, etc. I'd apply that to other things (Numina too, which IIRC Dhampir can learn) for similar reasons. <br /> <br /> Ultimately though it depends what you can work with other players and your storyteller, since they have to deal with the character in question. Level one powers might work fine for some people, and I can see some appeal in trying to make such limits work creatively. You might obtain power through breadth and diversity rather than depth (especially since Dhampir can still learn numina IIRC Time of Thin Blood. Or, more recently, become imbued due to BJD.) <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dogstar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1323053-revenant-mage-and-ghoul-merit?p=1329617#post1329617\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personally I've never understood why there's such an extreme bias against ghoul mages, on a purely balance level those couple of dots in disciplines are pretty useless for all but the most minmaxed of characters.<br /> <br /> It seems to me that the real problem is that one of the writers/game designers has a problem with them and has abused their position to screw this option over.</div> </div> </div> </div>What's interesting is that I initially figured that the 'Blood Treachery' writer either had a bad experience with powergaming or had been inundated with complaints about such and then put in the warning. Sort of like how they have all those 'this is fiction, not real life' disclaimers. But then I've recently noticed the same 'warnings' in Kindred of the East (no hybrids unless a 'cinematic' game) and also Time of Thin Blood (No Awakend or kinfolk, even though you can have kinfolk mages, fae-blooded mages, and ghoul or revenant mages. And Dhampir ARE Revenants) and much the same reason as in Blood Treachery ('its overpowered and this isn't that kind of game.') <br /> <br /> This seems to be idea behindthe WoD and the Storyteller system - they're about thematic narative - ie storytelling - rather than the 'cinematic'. Some of the systems handle these warnings well (Dark ages: Mage comes to mind. They convey that idea whilst acknowledging the potentially elitist attitude behind this) whilst others... don't (Blood Treachery and its disdain for Revenant/Ghoul Mages, Time of Thin Blood, etc.) and they come off as being overly judgemental -if not shaming - about the value 'cinematic' games.<br /> <br /> And that's where the disconnect comes from with Blood Treachery, Time of Thin Blood, etc. The idea behind the warnings is sound, but the execution of that warning just undermines the idea completely because it comes across as 'elitist' and like they have no trust in the players to handle such delicate topics themselves. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Possessed</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1323053-revenant-mage-and-ghoul-merit?p=1330247#post1330247\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah the treatment of Ghoul/Revenant Mages has always been rather odd, considering the cost of Ghoul Merit itself and the fact that you cannuse low cost Merits to build a \u201dbetter\u201d Ghoul Mage with all the same advantages and non of the downsides.</div> </div> </div> </div>Interesting idea. It does look like it seems to follow 'Dark Ages: Mage' rules rather than MTA rules. I was looking at that system as an alternative too, so its an interesting way to go about it. DA:M feels like its halfway between hedge magic and MTA's sphere-based True magick. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The greatest advantages Ghoul Mages get?<br /> 1) Slow (if Revenant) aging and fully stopped aging for salongin as you have a supply of Blood.<br /> <br /> Can be beaten by a 1 point Merit with no downsides.<br /> <br /> 2) Access to Disciplines. Mostly 1st level only, physical only unless aside from Family Disciplines (Revenants) or Domitor Disciplines and higher only if Domitor is low generation. Aside from Blood bond as a disadvantage these Disciplines cost 20xp for the 1st level and 15xcurrent for the rest if allowed.<br /> <br /> Many 1st level Discipline effects can be either replicated by Coincidental Magick or low cost Merits. Non of the downsides.<br /> <br /> 3) Finally one big advantage is that Magick that looks like traditional Vampire tricks is considered coincidental. This can be quite a big deal considering things associated with Vampires as it will allow some mind effects and even some transformation effects Etc.<br /> <br /> This is the major benefit that cannot really be replicated easily with other Merits but is it still worth the limitations to your Arete, Blood Bond and other disadvantages?<br /> So no Ghoul/Revenant Mages aren\u2019t, atleast in my personal opinion, overpowered even without the limit to Arete. Blood Bond and the general Vampire connection is likely quite enough of a disadvantage to Balance it out. But if you wish to use the official Ghoul/Revenant rules the Black Hand book is the way to go. </div> </div> </div>Again I think it depends on how you decide to write up the character, and what other players/Storytellers will be fine with. I agree that they're not any more inherently overpowered than any other character (including generic mage/Vampire/etc. builds) but you still have to treat it case-by-case. <br /> <br /> That's really why the topic interests me, because I find it interesting to puzzle over the concepts and see how they might work despite the fact some of the rulebooks have such a clear disdain for them. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25533-mister_dunpeal","name":"Mister_Dunpeal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"308"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-17T12:38:42-07:00","id":1331373,"title":"","content":"Half vampires meaning: Thin-Blood Vampire (Using 'Time of Thinblood book') who has had sex with a human person, and has given birth.<br /> <br /> The resulting child is a 'revenant ghoul'-type creature.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25315-jihelu","name":"Jihelu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"167"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-20T06:21:48-07:00","id":1331763,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mister_Dunpeal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1323053-revenant-mage-and-ghoul-merit?p=1331351#post1331351\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Eh. I don't think it's that big a problem by itself - it depends on how you handle it. Even if the particular game spans centuries it's not insurmountable. Heck part of the appeal for that is I see certain parallels between the quest for ascension and VtM's 'Golconda' quest. A struggle between the awakened avatar and the vampiric curse seems like it could be approached in a lot of interesting ways.</div> </div> </div> </div>If being a Ghoul it's a &quot;struggle&quot;. If it's something that has to be fought...that's a Flaw. Some Flaws grant you power, but it's always something you want to get rid off, something that causes or will cause more problems than it's worth. A &quot;struggle between the awakened avatar and the vampiric curse&quot; sounds a lot like that.<br /> <br /> That it can be interesting has nothing to do with it being a Merit or a Flaw. A Dark Fate can be approached in a lot of interesting ways and it's not a Merit...<br /> <br /> A Flaw has as much right to have appeal as a Merit. These exist to grant color to the character. Even more, I dare say, if a Flaw doesn't have appeal then taking it it's pure powergaming - if you don't want to roleplay the &quot;struggle&quot;, then why do you take a Flaw?, most of the time it's to get the extra freebies","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 08-20-2019, 09:24 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-23T16:06:14-07:00","id":1332382,"title":"","content":"One of my PCs is a Revenant and he doesn't know it. The only clues have been:<br /> <br /> - he heals pretty serious wounds very quickly, although both the character and the player have assumed this is a blessing of his Celtic moon goddess. His adoptive sister is a technocracy cyborg who can be hit by a frag grenade and shake it off like it was a hard punch to the gut (which is visibly knitting itself together if she did take damage), so his healing doesn't seem odd really.<br /> <br /> - he essentially just has Auspex 1 with the name filed off; the pretense is that it's a &quot;gift from the moon's serene light&quot;, and despite having played VtM the player doesn't seem to have noticed.<br /> <br /> Here are my thoughts on Ghoul and Revenant Mages:<br /> <br /> - The merits are described as though they are flaws in some places. Keep in mind that this merit costs 6 or 7 points; it should be an overall benefit. Powerful merits often have downsides, and being a Ghoul has plenty. Your domitor has their own agenda, and is a modern-day slave-owner who you are likely in love with. To them, you are often disposable. Without blood you will face withdrawal problems. Your aura is tainted by death, and this messes with your Resonance. All of this is ENOUGH. Vitae shouldn't be an Arete-Be-Gone formula. Avatar and Arete are highly spiritually significant, and shouldn't be destroyed by Vitae. Tainted? Oh, absolutely. But for some, Ghouldom could be the path to further Seekings, assuming their paradigm is compatible. If your Paradigm were &quot;All power comes from the blood&quot; or &quot;Mages draw power from monsters/spirits&quot; then that would make perfect sense.<br /> <br /> - Blood Treachery should be ignored. If you don't ignore it, laugh at it. The writer is a bad ST. Happy to say that with confidence. They write as though players are the *enemy* of storytellers. &quot;Here's what to do if any of your sneaky bastard players try to purchase a 6 point merit that you let them purchase... you FUCK THEM UP with this list of punishmen- I mean, drawbacks.&quot; If you don't want Ghouls, say no to Ghouls. If you let them be Ghouls, just follow the benefits and drawbacks written in the merit. Limiting their ability to do Seekings, eroding their Avatar, is ridiculous.<br /> <br /> - Disciplines are incredibly expensive for mortals, and far weaker than Magick by comparison (when cost, level limits and other issues are factored in), so if a PC wants to spend their EXP learning Quietus 1 or Fortitude 1 for example, more power to them. If you aren't using the VtM rules then just stick to the automatic success on physical damage benefit. Your game will NOT be ruined by a Hermetic Mage with Lure of Flames 1, trust me.<br /> <br /> So yeah, the tl;dr is to be supportive of your players. If you don't want something at your table, politely say no. If you let them do something, don't seek childish revenge over it. Being a Ghoul or Revenant can add a lot to a character in terms of drama and flavour and magickal identity, so I'd recommend it if the player seems like they're the right type to handle it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-23T22:33:09-07:00","id":1332417,"title":"","content":"If any thaumaturgy path, fucking level 1, ruins your game in MAGE I will personally come to your house and let you kill me with your bare hands.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25315-jihelu","name":"Jihelu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"167"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-26T15:59:00-07:00","id":1332744,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jihelu</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1323053-revenant-mage-and-ghoul-merit?p=1332417#post1332417\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If any thaumaturgy path, fucking level 1, ruins your game in MAGE I will personally come to your house and let you kill me with your bare hands.</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay I gave this some thought and I could ALMOST see Mercury as an exception to this, since even in Mage paradox-free teleportation powered by Willpower is a big deal. BUT even then that's not a game-breaking deal (teleporting 10 feet is a very neat and powerful trick, but it isn't much use for transport), and Mercury is an ultra-rare path that you couldn't really justify a Ghoul learning. A far better example would be the Sorcery path, which is less expensive and doesn't remain limited in terms of level.<br /> <br /> Plus at the end of the day the ST is simply allowed to say &quot;no, Mages can't choose to learn this static stuff under normal circumstances&quot; or &quot;it's possible, but it isn't happening to PCs at this table&quot; or &quot;JESUS CHRIST BUY A DOT OF ARETE IT PUTS YOU CLOSER TO OMNISCIENCE&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 08-26-2019, 07:02 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-26T19:42:52-07:00","id":1332767,"title":"","content":"That teleporting requires 1 point of blood (Your max being like, 2 points unless you are a revenant) and using the sorcery path requires a point of willpower + some turns and shit.<br /> <br /> I think its a valid price to pay for paradox free mini teleport.<br /> <br /> Also: If you have the ghoul merit anyway you can teleport coincidentally using true magick as long as it 'fits the theme of vampires'. So you could disappear in a cloud of mist or some shit and teleport under/through doors.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25315-jihelu","name":"Jihelu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"167"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-26T23:35:58-07:00","id":1332781,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jihelu</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1323053-revenant-mage-and-ghoul-merit?p=1332767#post1332767\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also: If you have the ghoul merit anyway you can teleport coincidentally using true magick as long as it 'fits the theme of vampires'. So you could disappear in a cloud of mist or some shit and teleport under/through doors.</div> </div> </div> </div>So kinfolk Mages doing &quot;Gifts&quot; and Werewolf stuff coincidentally, and Ghouls doing &quot;Vampire&quot; stuff coincidentally... I was under the impression that this was coincidental when they were surrounded by nightfolk. Is it just constant? Does it depend on the reality zone?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-27T04:23:06-07:00","id":1332801,"title":"","content":"My general rule of thumb is, if it isn't derived from Arete it doesn't cause Paradox. It can still cause issues (like breaking the Masquerade or the Veil) but you won't be doxed for using Protean 1 (for example).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imageshack.com/i/j5vvlqp\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/j5vvlqp\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/ghw20supporterhiddengaroup\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/n18segp\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0v25ewp\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#404040\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">English is not my native language, so i apologize for errors in grammar or spelling.</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/392-cadmiumcadamium","name":"Cadmiumcadamium","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar392_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"936"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-27T07:45:41-07:00","id":1332814,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1323053-revenant-mage-and-ghoul-merit?p=1332781#post1332781\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> So kinfolk Mages doing &quot;Gifts&quot; and Werewolf stuff coincidentally, and Ghouls doing &quot;Vampire&quot; stuff coincidentally... I was under the impression that this was coincidental when they were surrounded by nightfolk. Is it just constant? Does it depend on the reality zone?</div> </div> </div> </div>It was something that came out of the TBH book (OR the book that has the TBH in it and talks about ghoul mages) where it says any 'traditional (Key word I think, or something similar) vampire effects you do are coincidental'<br /> <br /> Though it says traditional (OR the functional equivalent) so just because YOU KNOW that vampires can flesh mend, doesn't mean flesh mending becomes coincidental for you....you'd have to do something like turning into mist, etc.<br /> <br /> It doesn't say you need vampires/other nightfolk surrounding you for this to take effect. So having the blood in you (IE: Ghoul) is enough.<br /> <br /> Not sure if kinfolk mages get their stuff coincidental, as I don't think the text touches on it. I'd probably let it as it isn't too powerful.<br /> <br /> I think the Nephalim merit in revised clarified you could do stuff related to your parent (Angel/Demon) and it was coincidental, but in M20 they threw it out/forgot about it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25315-jihelu","name":"Jihelu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"167"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-27T13:49:31-07:00","id":1332878,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cadmiumcadamium</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1323053-revenant-mage-and-ghoul-merit?p=1332801#post1332801\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My general rule of thumb is, if it isn't derived from Arete it doesn't cause Paradox. It can still cause issues (like breaking the Masquerade or the Veil) but you won't be doxed for using Protean 1 (for example).</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh no we're talking about a Ghoul using Forces or Life to imitate Protean 1, and trying to make it coincidental by going &quot;well, Vampires&quot;.<br /> <br /> Naturally if a Mage is actually using a Gift, or a Discipline, or a Sorcery Path etc. then that never invokes Paradox, since it's part of static reality.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-27T14:19:16-07:00","id":1332887,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jihelu</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1323053-revenant-mage-and-ghoul-merit?p=1331373#post1331373\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Half vampires meaning: Thin-Blood Vampire (Using 'Time of Thinblood book') who has had sex with a human person, and has given birth.<br /> <br /> The resulting child is a 'revenant ghoul'-type creature.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> So then Dhampir. Well we have imbued Dhampir, and we know Revenant Mages were a thing in Blood Treachery and now in V20's black hand (I do like that application better as I said) so I see no reason why you couldn't have Dhampir-mages. <br /> <br /> Interestingly enough TTB mentions under the character creation rules pertaining to 'magical' disicplines that the Vitae in Caitiff, Thin bloods and Dhampir make them especially suited for learning linear magic (and presumably psychic powers) as well as developing their own 'magical' disciplines. And they're also functionally the same as Revenants. <br /> <br /> It's actually interesting they note all this (when at the same time discouraging 'crossover' characters on the basis of power, go figure) because it's a good stepping stone for creating a Dhampir-Mage. <br /> <br /> They'd almost certainly be Orphans, or maybe taken in by the Hollow Ones. This is another great case for either a struggling avatar or 'sleepwalker' status, too. And if you use the V20 Black Hand approach to Revenant-Mages it dovetails nicely with the 'Dark Age: Mage' approach to magic as opposed to the more 'modern' sphere-based. 'Ancient' magic seems like something that would more easily develop from hedge magic than the later sphere based systems as well.<br /> <br /> For a Revenant/Dhampir mage we could consider that the blood AND disciplines help as foci for the magic (something akin to rotes which are themselves close to linear magic.) A Dhampir-Mage (or Revenant Mage) could have potential to develop a more versatile sphere-based magic (and to move beyond rotes or more purely coincidental magic) but they might need a teacher willing to do that. <br /> <br /> So progression might be: Linear magic (And disciplines) + Struggling awake merit from Sorcerer. This can progress to an 'awakened' Dhampir who uses Dark Ages:mage style magic (foundations and pillars) in accordance with V20 rules on Revenant-Mages) and with the appropriate merit for Revenant-Mages. They develop as Orphans otherwise (other tweaks like 'sleepwalker' optional, but I do think this fits with the Orphan mold too) until they acquire a teacher of sorts where they might progress to sphere type magic. <br /> <br /> Even if one keeps it restricted more to discipline-based 'magic' you can adapt alchemy in various ideas (a great source might be borrowing the ideas of thin blooded alchemy from V5 for options, merge it with the hedge magic alchemy rules outlined in Sorcerer Revised and a number of others.) Anarchs also have their own sorcery (another place where a Dhampir might learn it) as well. Hedge magic might even represent a source of learning too (and of course some linear magic is surprisingly effective even if it lacks much of the versatility of true magick.) <br /> <br /> I think its something that is still interesting to play around with mechanically (even the 'toxic effects of blood' from Blood Treachery) just because there are so many interesting ways you COULD play around with it. Like I took a peek at the Dead Magic rules for Liches and they had some really interesting ideas that differed in some ways from Blood Treachery (You can raise spheres as a Liche, but not Arete as I recall) - some of those rationales actually apply better to Ghouls than what Blood Treachery did, but it also got me thinking that there are other ways to interpret those rules relative to Revenants (and its not exactly like they were spelled out to begin with. Initially I assumed the effect was linear... but if it's NOT linear it could lead to very different effects that aren't quite so 'permanent') And if you decide to throw V20's black hand supplement into the mix (and there's no reason you have to take blood treachery exclusively over V20's black hand book) things get even more interesting. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1323053-revenant-mage-and-ghoul-merit?p=1331763#post1331763\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If being a Ghoul it's a &quot;struggle&quot;. If it's something that has to be fought...that's a Flaw. Some Flaws grant you power, but it's always something you want to get rid off, something that causes or will cause more problems than it's worth. A &quot;struggle between the awakened avatar and the vampiric curse&quot; sounds a lot like that.<br /> <br /> That it can be interesting has nothing to do with it being a Merit or a Flaw. A Dark Fate can be approached in a lot of interesting ways and it's not a Merit...<br /> <br /> A Flaw has as much right to have appeal as a Merit. These exist to grant color to the character. Even more, I dare say, if a Flaw doesn't have appeal then taking it it's pure powergaming - if you don't want to roleplay the &quot;struggle&quot;, then why do you take a Flaw?, most of the time it's to get the extra freebies</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I'm... not sure what your point is? You seem to be taking a highly 'mechanistic' approach to the concept, which isn't really relevant to what you quoted since that reply was an essentially 'non-mechanistic Storyteller can use it how they like' approach. If you're trying to say 'this is a difference of opinion agree to disagree' that's fine, since I don't see any way it can be proved one way or another but I feel that trying to fixate overly on the mechanics kinda misses the point. Especially when the original complaint was that the 'warnings' against Revenants were themselves needlessly mechanic-centric and undermined the narrative-driven rationale for making such a caution to begin with. <br /> <br /> Basically there's no way mechanics can fundamentally overturn narrative under the guidance of a competent Storyteller and reasonable players, and thus even 'hybrid' concepts that some WW authors seemingly despised can be perfectly workable in a thematic frame despite their potential for abuse or more 'cinematic' play. <br /> <br /> And if that wasn't enough for me, I actually think it's a good challenge to 'beat them at their own game' so to speak by working with the rules even if I don't agree how they were implemented or the mindset behind them. Its not like I'm FORCED to adhere to them..... <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1323053-revenant-mage-and-ghoul-merit?p=1332781#post1332781\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> So kinfolk Mages doing &quot;Gifts&quot; and Werewolf stuff coincidentally, and Ghouls doing &quot;Vampire&quot; stuff coincidentally... I was under the impression that this was coincidental when they were surrounded by nightfolk. Is it just constant? Does it depend on the reality zone?</div> </div> </div> </div>I think when it comes to 'consensus' and 'paradox' there is a certain amount of context. Consensus is basically 'what people believe is true'. It doesn't mean 'science and logic' necessarily. Consensus can also cover superstitions and personal beliefs in a 'local' sense if the belief is widespread enough. <br /> <br /> Like if you have alot of religious belief of some kind strong in an area, and it could be explained away as 'a miracle' then it might be treated as coincidental. Similar to how linear magic because of how it works might be coincidental. Similarly the 'powers' of vampires, fae, or garou/kinfolk, etc. would be treated as 'coincidental' because they're akin to static magic and in many respects are informed by said beliefs. ( I also suspect this is why Vampires, werewolves, etc. are not considered 'bygones' compared to alot of other mythical creatures. Although by the End Times that may have changed, which could explain things like the Withering. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25533-mister_dunpeal","name":"Mister_Dunpeal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"308"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-27T15:38:50-07:00","id":1332908,"title":"","content":"Liches can't raise their Arete anymore but they can raise their spheres to their current Arete. They also completely can not ascend. <br /> <br /> The max sphere they need is also only a 4, so you can technically be a lich with 4 arete. However usually example characters have higher arete than spheres. So someone with a max 4 in a sphere might have 7 arete, so you could have a lich with 8 arete and 5 in all spheres.<br /> <br /> Usually people become a Lich though because it takes a lot of effort/power to live forever in a way that doesn't suck, hence why they rely on a method that uses 4 in a sphere, but you can extend your life pretty long with just life.<br /> <br /> Also it would be fun to think of the Withering in a Mage perspective of Vampires finally getting Paradox, god's curse on Caine has finally been so shadowed by man's power and consensus that they finally start to die.<br /> (That's how I'm using it as an example, not that I think thats how it should be. Just a fun 'explanation' that people could use. Also one vampires wouldn't agree with. &quot;What's Paradox? What's consensus? I don't care I'm dying!&quot;)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25315-jihelu","name":"Jihelu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"167"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-28T04:37:52-07:00","id":1332955,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1323053-revenant-mage-and-ghoul-merit?p=1332878#post1332878\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Oh no we're talking about a Ghoul using Forces or Life to imitate Protean 1, and trying to make it coincidental by going &quot;well, Vampires&quot;.<br /> <br /> Naturally if a Mage is actually using a Gift, or a Discipline, or a Sorcery Path etc. then that never invokes Paradox, since it's part of static reality.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> My apologies. But you can always flip my previous answer aroud. If it's derived from Arete, it can potentially cause Paradox.<br /> IMO, you'll get bitchslapped by paradox if you use Magick to turn into a cloud of Mist, even if you're surrounded by Gangrel.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imageshack.com/i/j5vvlqp\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/j5vvlqp\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/ghw20supporterhiddengaroup\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/n18segp\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0v25ewp\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#404040\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">English is not my native language, so i apologize for errors in grammar or spelling.</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/392-cadmiumcadamium","name":"Cadmiumcadamium","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar392_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"936"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-08-28T05:52:56-07:00","id":1332960,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mister_Dunpeal</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm... not sure what your point is? You seem to be taking a highly 'mechanistic' approach to the concept, which isn't really relevant to what you quoted since that reply was an essentially 'non-mechanistic Storyteller can use it how they like' approach.<br /> <br /> ...<br /> <br /> Basically there's no way mechanics can fundamentally overturn narrative under the guidance of a competent Storyteller and reasonable players, and thus even 'hybrid' concepts that some WW authors seemingly despised can be perfectly workable in a thematic frame despite their potential for abuse or more 'cinematic' play.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm taking a &quot;game design&quot; approach that englobes both mechanistic and non mechanistic approaches.<br /> <br /> Games have rules to help the narrative. A roleplayer doesn't really need <b>any </b>rule to play (as children prove all the time), but usually RPG have mechanics to guide the narration. That's the place mechanics have in my approach: They're the tools of a Storyteller.<br /> <br /> Within this context a good Storyteller can work with any set of rules no matter how devious in the same vein as a good chef can cook good food with any kitchen implements (perhaps even with none). That's not to say that a good chef wouldn't <i>like </i>to have the best implements or wouldn't find easier to cook great food having them.<br /> <br /> That STs can work with what they have at hand it's no good reason to defend a mechanic that's not helpfull because STs could work better with better rules. Hence why you see so many STs in this very thread talking against that rule. It's not that it can't be &quot;gamed&quot;, there's simply no need to game it when it's easier to ignore it.<br /> <br /> More so, modifying the rule so it serves it's narrative purpose better happens to be easy too, why not do it then?. You yourself are proposing a lot of house rules for the Revenants when you say to apply this or that system that isn't really ruled for them. Well, this is no different. If you like the drama of your power withering in front of the Curse ('non-mechanisistic' ), and that's represented better as a Flaw ('mechanistic'), why not make it a Flaw?.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And if that wasn't enough for me, I actually think it's a good challenge to 'beat them at their own game' so to speak by working with the rules even if I don't agree how they were implemented or the mindset behind them. </div> </div> </div>I have nothing against wanting to beat the system for sports, but as ST I fail to see the advantage of doing so in this case.<br /> <br /> From the point I'm playing it isn't &quot;their game&quot; anymore but mine (Rule Zero). Hence I feel free to challenge their reasonings when making the rules and propose rules of my own. There's no rules police that's going to chace after you for proposing rules, hence I don't see why anyone should beat anyone at anyone's game.<br /> <br /> If the OP wants to swim against the current for whatever reason (maybe he's not the ST and has a very strict ST that would never change the rules, for instance. We don't know), then sure, the best way it's to buy the Merit when playing a short story (like a One Shot). There the added disadvantages of Blood Treachery will never have time to show up, as you said.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 08-28-2019, 09:07 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}